xnu 0305: the panel's brightness, a DCS write on the frame that starts it

The screen sits at whatever level the bootloader left it: iOS sets brightness
through IOMobileFramebuffer, the port never sent it, and on this panel the
backlight is a DCS long write (command 0x51, 12-bit DBV) that nothing
produced. Both the value and the way it leaves come from the shipped kernel:
AppleCLCD::vsyncFired was the only place that transmitted a level, the packet
is three bytes, and it goes out as a DSI DMA from a command page in memory.

The level, the format and the command byte live next to the DSI window.
"bl LEVEL [FMT [CMD]]" on the USB control channel stores them and the next
vsync sends them, so a slider costs one packet per frame instead of one per
command. The write is the old sequence register for register: TPG FIFO reset,
0x50000000 (0x54000000 when format bit 4 is set) into
DSI_COMMAND_MODE_DMA_CTRL, the page's physical address into
DSI_DMA_CMD_OFFSET, eight bytes on the wire, trigger, then a 100 ms wait for
DMA done with one retry after the same reset.

The command page has to be a low one, and it is not allocated. DSI_DMA_CMD_OFFSET
is 32 bits wide and the kernel's window starts at 0x840000000, so no allocator
can serve an address the engine can reach; the page is the last 64 KiB of the
splash carve-out at 0xb8000000, the only memory below 4 GiB this port owns and
one the panel never scans. mondrianblbuf still overrides it, and the
write/read canary the old kernel used still refuses a page that is not RAM. No
level is hardcoded either -- mondrianbl (default 1024 of 4095) and
mondrianblfmt (default 8) choose the boot level, and the panel only receives it
once the kick has started the interface under it.

"dcs CMD [P0 [P1 [P2]]]" sends one packet from the control thread through the
inline path instead of the frame's DMA (0x05 with no parameter, 0x39 with up
to three), which is how a panel register is poked without waiting for a tick.
Nothing in present() or swap() changed; in steady state the whole feature
costs the frame path one bool test.

